1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,173
A school keeps its records in two spreadsheets: one in the office, one in the exam section.

2
00:00:06,173 --> 00:00:08,568
Both list Anisha, from Kathmandu.

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:10,455
She moves to Bhaktapur.

4
00:00:10,455 --> 00:00:12,447
The office updates its copy.

5
00:00:12,447 --> 00:00:14,843
Nobody tells the exam section.

6
00:00:14,843 --> 00:00:20,325
Now the school has two different answers to one question, and no way to know which is right.

7
00:00:20,325 --> 00:00:23,955
That is what goes wrong when the same data is stored in many places.

8
00:00:24,167 --> 00:00:31,734
A database management system stores each fact once, shares it with everyone, and answers any question you can ask.

9
00:00:31,734 --> 00:00:33,086
This unit shows you how.

10
00:00:38,567 --> 00:00:43,921
Welcome to Class 12 Computer Science, Unit 1: Database Management System.

11
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:47,486
Seven parts.

12
00:00:47,486 --> 00:00:50,755
Data, databases and the DBMS.

13
00:00:50,755 --> 00:00:52,994
Fields, records and keys.

14
00:00:52,994 --> 00:00:55,729
SQL for defining and changing data.

15
00:00:55,729 --> 00:00:57,135
Joins and grouping.

16
00:00:57,135 --> 00:00:58,789
The database models.

17
00:00:58,789 --> 00:01:00,130
Normalisation.

18
00:01:00,130 --> 00:01:02,776
And distributed databases and security.

19
00:01:07,133 --> 00:01:11,627
Data are raw facts: a name, a number, a date.

20
00:01:11,627 --> 00:01:13,671
On their own they mean nothing.

21
00:01:13,671 --> 00:01:18,413
Processed into a sentence that helps someone decide, they become information.

22
00:01:19,633 --> 00:01:26,028
A database is an organised collection of related data, shared by many users and programs.

23
00:01:26,028 --> 00:01:31,796
The DBMS is the software that manages it, such as MySQL or Oracle.

24
00:01:31,796 --> 00:01:43,553
And a database system is the whole arrangement: hardware, software, the data itself, the users, including the database administrator, and the procedures they follow.

25
00:01:44,767 --> 00:01:49,378
The ANSI SPARC architecture splits a database into three levels.

26
00:01:49,378 --> 00:01:53,323
At the top, each user sees their own external view.

27
00:01:53,323 --> 00:02:00,185
In the middle, the conceptual level describes everything: all the entities and how they relate.

28
00:02:00,185 --> 00:02:04,009
At the bottom, the internal level is how it actually sits on disk.

29
00:02:04,233 --> 00:02:08,844
Because the levels are separate, you can change one without breaking the others.

30
00:02:08,844 --> 00:02:15,003
Add a new column, and existing views still work: logical data independence.

31
00:02:15,003 --> 00:02:22,421
Add an index or move to a faster disk, and the schema does not change: physical data independence.

32
00:02:23,633 --> 00:02:26,825
Compare the old file system with a DBMS.

33
00:02:26,825 --> 00:02:31,825
Files repeat data and drift apart; a DBMS stores it once.

34
00:02:31,825 --> 00:02:38,361
Files need a new program for every new question; a DBMS answers any query.

35
00:02:38,361 --> 00:02:44,025
It enforces rules, handles many users at once, and controls who sees what.

36
00:02:44,025 --> 00:02:48,104
The price: cost, complexity, and a trained administrator.

37
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:56,248
Data in a relational database lives in tables.

38
00:02:56,248 --> 00:03:02,172
A field is one column: one property, with one data type, like faculty.

39
00:03:02,172 --> 00:03:05,719
A record is one row: everything about one student.

40
00:03:06,933 --> 00:03:09,018
Keys identify rows.

41
00:03:09,018 --> 00:03:13,575
Here no two students share a roll number, a symbol number or an email.

42
00:03:13,575 --> 00:03:19,174
Each of these alone is unique, and cannot be made smaller: they are the candidate keys.

43
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:24,454
The designer picks one as the primary key: roll, short and stable.

44
00:03:24,454 --> 00:03:26,810
It can never be empty or repeated.

45
00:03:26,810 --> 00:03:29,415
The others become alternate keys.

46
00:03:29,415 --> 00:03:36,863
Add any extra column, like roll with name, and it is still unique but not minimal: a super key.

47
00:03:36,863 --> 00:03:44,857
And a foreign key is a column whose values must already exist as a primary key in another table: that is what links tables.

48
00:03:46,067 --> 00:03:51,043
A common mistake: the primary key is not simply the first column.

49
00:03:51,043 --> 00:03:55,821
It is a rule the DBMS enforces: unique and never null.

50
00:03:55,821 --> 00:03:59,682
And a key made of two columns together is still one primary key.

51
00:04:04,467 --> 00:04:06,239
SQL has two halves.

52
00:04:06,239 --> 00:04:13,075
The data definition language builds the structure: CREATE a table, with each column's type and constraints.

53
00:04:13,075 --> 00:04:16,650
Roll is the primary key; name may not be empty.

54
00:04:16,867 --> 00:04:20,723
DDL also has ALTER, DROP and TRUNCATE.

55
00:04:20,723 --> 00:04:26,908
The data manipulation language works with the rows: SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE.

56
00:04:26,908 --> 00:04:32,851
Do not mix up DROP, which deletes the whole table, with DELETE, which removes chosen rows.

57
00:04:34,067 --> 00:04:35,332
Retrieving data.

58
00:04:35,332 --> 00:04:42,207
Select the roll, name and district from students, where the faculty is Science, ordered by name.

59
00:04:42,207 --> 00:04:44,297
Watch what the query does to the table.

60
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:49,853
WHERE keeps only the rows that pass the test: the two Management students drop out.

61
00:04:49,853 --> 00:04:54,463
SELECT keeps only the columns named, so faculty goes too.

62
00:04:54,463 --> 00:04:58,063
Three rows remain, already in alphabetical order.

63
00:04:59,267 --> 00:05:00,414
More queries.

64
00:05:00,414 --> 00:05:02,328
IN tests against a list.

65
00:05:02,328 --> 00:05:05,089
DISTINCT removes repeated values.

66
00:05:05,089 --> 00:05:13,123
The aggregate functions, count, sum, average, max and min, collapse many rows into one value.

67
00:05:13,123 --> 00:05:21,373
INSERT adds a row, UPDATE changes values, and DELETE removes rows, each with a WHERE clause to pick which ones.

68
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:28,433
The most dangerous line in SQL: an UPDATE or DELETE with no WHERE clause.

